the reverend dr martin luther king jr's  daughter  bernice is calling on congress to  restore voting rights protections  now it's a cause that was championed by  congressman john lewis  she made the comments at the lewis  funeral yesterday  as we honor the life of congressman john  lewis  who shed blood on that edmund pres  pettis bridge  that we might have the right to vote  grant that we  never again take that right for granted  and that we exercise it no matter what  and that we never again tamper with that  right  overtaking this hour our congress  that they might restore voting rights  protections  in our nation lewis as you know  died earlier this month after fighting  pancreatic cancer  wesley lowery is a correspondent for 60  and six  and he wrote a lengthy obituary for  lewis in gq magazine  60 in six features 60-minute style  storytelling  and around six-minute episodes it airs  on the streaming at quibby  and is produced by cbs news which is a  part of viacom cbs an investor in quibby  wesley good morning it seems every time  you come on i'm raving about a piece you  wrote  and here i go again about that gq piece  it was so beautifully written i thought  you captured him so brilliantly  because of all the people you talked to  but in part you wrote this  you said john lewis quote is a breathing  civil rights monument  and that he was a bridge for civil  rights from the civil rights movement to  the contemporary world  an enduring reminder of how the nation's  past shapes its presence  shapes its present i read that and i  went go you go let wes  lowry you go so after all the people  that you've talked to because i really  think you captured the man and his  mission  after all the people you talked to and  you talked to many people in his life  how do you sum him up  certainly you know i think that you know  john lewis  in many ways was someone with a real  integrity  um of spirit and of mission a real true  believer  in uh nonviolent philosophy um but also  in agitating to try to change the  country and make it better  and you know i think that you know  that sentence that you highlighted this  idea that he's kind of in many ways or  he was  a living monument but but also right  there can be a temptation with that to  you know almost turn him into a santa  claus figure right well that's john  lewis he did that thing  so many years ago but the fact that he  was still with us was a reminder that  these struggles weren't that long ago  right that that we're not that far  removed from the civil rights era that's  not to suggest that there haven't been  major changes and that  we we aren't getting better on these  things but it was this reminder that you  know i think sometimes with history we  like to pretend that things were  longer ago than they were because we  would never do such a thing or how could  we  and john lewis was a reminder that this  fight kind of was still going on because  here's this boy who who was beaten on  the edmond penis bridge for the right to  vote and if he's still alive  if he's still here with us then that  couldn't have been so long ago  yeah hey wes did you ever get a chance  to meet him by chance during your  travels did you ever spend any time with  him  certainly yeah i spent some time with  john lewis when i actually first got to  the washington post years ago i was  covering congress primarily and so  i i would run into congressman lewis uh  just in the hallways  you know and have conversations and talk  and you know we weren't uh particularly  close there were many reporters and  colleagues of mine who were closer to  him than i was but  but i knew john lewis and had interacted  with him and and he was just he was  someone who always  took the time to talk to have  conversations there was a humility that  kind of  radiated off of him in a way that isn't  always true of our elected officials  right it isn't always true  frankly even of other great people and  that was something that was so  particular  yeah listen we all treasure those times  now they  they take on to me a very different  meaning and his final words to us in his  new york times op-ed he says  the most powerful non-violent change  agent we have  what's at stake he's he said the voting  he was talking about voting it's the  most powerful non-violent change agent  we have  what's at stake do you think in the  fight against voter suppression it's a  very serious thing coming up with the  election  in november it certainly  is you know and we saw both what  representative lewis wrote in his final  words and then what  uh bernice king and then later barack  obama said in their eulogies yesterday  right that this fight over access to the  ballot box is real  it's something that's still going on for  a lot of people it's not a settled  question and so there's a lot at stake  there right  we still have major questions to answer  right about voting rights in places like  dc or puerto rico or  should we do automatic registration or a  federal holiday  and so you know this is not a fight  that's over and i think that that's  something that  uh representative lewis talked about  often right and i think one of the  things he said  and he wrote this in this in the piece  was at times there can be this  sense that you know people feel as if  they voted and it hasn't paid off for  them or  they feel disenchanted but he noted that  you know in a democracy  the vote is the most powerful tool um  and he was urging  a lot of the young activists and  protesters he supported them and  supported the work they were doing in  the streets but he was urging them to  show up in november as well  yeah and he's certainly passed on the  baton to the next generation of  activists  what do you think that they can learn  from him briefly  certainly you know look i think that  it's a resilience  you know john lewis was beaten to within  inches of his life  not just once not just on the bridge  many other times and  and and so i know i talk to a lot of  activists and they get discouraged at  times but i think that you know  uh john lewis is a representation of  uh you know getting knocked down and  getting back up uh because if  if the thing you're fighting for is  worth it you got to keep getting back up  yeah i marvel that he was never that he  was never bitter he was always very very  brave thank you wes  laurie for your time can't wait to read  your next piece thanks a lot  you can watch 60 and six on the mobile  streaming app quibby  congressman john lewis's legacy of good  trouble inspires us all  so we at cbs are very excited to honor  him with a very unique tribute and prime  time special featuring  some of the biggest names in  entertainment so how did this idea  came about oprah and tyler as in winfrey  and perry went to viacom cbs and pitched  the idea viacom cbs said yes  then they invited brad pitt to be one of  the hosts he said yes  then they invited me to be one of the  hosts you know i said yes  our focus is on the deep sense of  empathy and justice  that guided louis which all of us strive  to echo these days it features very  special performances by artists like  common and john legend here's a first  look  that's why we walk through america with  our hands up  when they go down we woman and man up  they say stay down  and we stand up shots we on the ground  the camera panned up  king pointed to the mountaintop and we  ran up
 

